COMPLETE PLAN — SYNTHETIC RFC 822 COMMUNICATION GENERATION & SCREENING TESTING PLATFORM

1. PURPOSE

Build a web application that takes existing journaled email/TXT communication as templates, understands their structure and communication patterns, and generates realistic synthetic employee conversations for testing an email-screening/monitoring system.

The system should generate four primary categories:

1. Business-sensitive / suspicious communication
2. Communication-platform switching
3. Personal communication that looks suspicious but is actually harmless
4. Normal/non-alerting communication

The generated communication must preserve the structural characteristics of the source emails while creating completely new content.

2. INPUT

The application accepts a directory or uploaded collection of TXT files containing RFC 822-formatted emails.

The files may contain:

* Normal emails
* Journaled emails
* Emails containing an inner/original email
* Standalone emails
* Reply conversations
* Forwarded conversations
* Multi-person conversations
* CC-based conversations
* Different email formats and structures

3. PRIMARY PROCESSING RULE

For every input email:

IF an inner/original email exists:

* Extract the inner email.
* Analyze the inner email.
* Determine its participants and conversation structure.
* Generate the synthetic communication inside the inner email.
* Preserve the outer journal/envelope structure.
* Place the newly generated inner email back into the same overall structure.

IF an inner/original email does NOT exist:

* Analyze the outer email directly.
* Generate the synthetic communication in the outer email.
* Preserve its RFC 822 structure.

This should be a hard rule in the application.

4. EMAIL STRUCTURE ANALYZER

The first stage should parse the RFC 822 structure.

It should identify:

* From
* To
* CC
* BCC, if available
* Subject
* Date
* Message-ID
* In-Reply-To
* References
* MIME information
* Attachments/attachment metadata where relevant
* Body
* Quoted replies
* Forwarded content
* Nested/original message
* Journal metadata

The original source files should remain read-only.

5. INNER EMAIL DETECTION

The application needs a dedicated component that determines whether the journaled message contains an original/inner email.

Possible structure:

Outer journal
|
+– Journal metadata
|
+– Original/inner email
|
+– From
+– To
+– Subject
+– Body

If detected:

Processing target = INNER EMAIL

Otherwise:

Processing target = OUTER EMAIL

The application should record which level was processed.

6. CONVERSATION ANALYSIS

For every communication, determine:

* Number of participants
* Unique participants
* Sender/recipient relationships
* Internal vs external participants
* Number of messages
* Thread depth
* Reply sequence
* Forward chain
* CC pattern
* Conversation length
* Message length
* Communication style
* Formal vs informal language
* Business vs personal characteristics
* One-to-one vs group communication

7. CONVERSATION TOPOLOGY

The system should understand who communicates with whom.

For example:

A -> B
B -> A
A -> B
B -> A

This is a two-person conversation.

Another:

A -> B
B -> A
A -> C
C -> A
A -> B

This is a three-person conversation with a particular participation pattern.

The generated conversation should maintain a similar structural pattern.

The system should not take a three-person source and randomly produce a one-person email.

8. TEMPLATE PROFILING

Every source conversation should be converted into a template profile.

Example:

Template ID:
TEMPLATE_001

Participants:
3

Message count:
7

Conversation type:
Group reply thread

CC:
Yes

Forward:
No

Thread depth:
6

Style:
Informal business

Average message length:
Medium

This profile becomes the structural blueprint for generation.

9. ANONYMIZATION

Before using real communication as a generation template, remove or abstract identifiable information.

For example:

Real employee:
john.smith@company.com

Internal representation:

EMPLOYEE_A

Another:

jane.doe@company.com

becomes:

EMPLOYEE_B

Real customer/company/project information should similarly be abstracted where appropriate.

The system should use the original communication for structure and style, not reproduce the original sensitive information.

10. ALERT CONCEPT INPUT

The user provides alert-triggering words, phrases, concepts, or rules.

Example:

leak announcement
customer data
confidential information
source code
credentials
financial information

The system should support both literal and semantic interpretation.

For example:

“leak announcement”

should not mean that the generator simply inserts the phrase “leak announcement.”

It should understand related concepts such as:

* unauthorized disclosure
* sharing restricted information
* getting information outside the organization
* coordinating an unauthorized disclosure
* discussing how to release restricted information

11. SCENARIO PLANNING ENGINE

Before generating an email, the application should create an internal scenario plan.

Example:

Concept:
Leak announcement

Category:
Business Sensitive

Behavior:
Employees coordinating unauthorized disclosure

Participants:
3

Messages:
6

Difficulty:
Hard

Literal trigger:
No

Semantic trigger:
Yes

Platform switching:
Yes

This planning stage is extremely important.

The AI should first decide WHAT should happen and then generate HOW the employees communicate about it.

12. BEHAVIOR LIBRARY

Create a reusable library of suspicious communication behaviors.

Examples:

* Unauthorized information sharing
* Restricted data transfer
* Attempting to bypass approved channels
* Requesting sensitive information
* Providing sensitive information
* Coordinating unauthorized disclosure
* Concealing activity
* Moving communication outside approved channels
* Attempting to involve unauthorized recipients

The behavior library should be mapped to alert concepts.

13. FOUR PRIMARY GENERATION CATEGORIES

CATEGORY 1 — BUSINESS-SENSITIVE

Employees themselves are participating in suspicious activity.

They should not merely be discussing an incident that happened elsewhere.

Examples of scenarios:

* Employees sharing restricted information
* Employees coordinating unauthorized disclosure
* Employees requesting sensitive information
* Employees transferring information through unauthorized channels
* Employees attempting to conceal the activity

The expected result should normally be:

ALERT

CATEGORY 2 — COMMUNICATION-PLATFORM SWITCHING

Employees move a potentially sensitive discussion away from the monitored channel.

Examples:

* Moving to personal email
* Moving to messaging applications
* Asking to discuss by phone
* Avoiding the current email thread
* Suggesting another communication channel

The system should generate both suspicious and contextual variations.

CATEGORY 3 — PERSONAL SUSPICIOUS / HARD NEGATIVE

The conversation contains language that could look suspicious but is actually personal and harmless.

Examples:

* Birthday surprises
* Personal financial discussions
* Travel
* Family matters
* Personal documents
* Wedding arrangements

Expected result:

NO ALERT

This category is critical for measuring false positives.

CATEGORY 4 — NORMAL

Completely ordinary communication.

Can be:

* Business
* Personal
* Mixed

Expected result:

NO ALERT

14. SEMANTIC VARIATION

Each concept should generate multiple forms:

* Exact keyword
* Synonym
* Paraphrase
* Semantic equivalent
* Indirect wording
* Implicit meaning
* Contextual meaning

This prevents the test dataset from becoming a simple keyword test.

15. DIFFICULTY LEVEL

Support:

Easy
Medium
Hard
Very Hard

Easy:
Obvious suspicious communication.

Medium:
Paraphrased concept.

Hard:
Indirect language.

Very Hard:
Suspicious intent becomes clear only when the entire conversation is considered.

16. MULTI-MESSAGE CONTEXT

The system should not put the entire suspicious behavior into one message.

Some conversations should develop naturally:

Message 1:
Normal discussion.

Message 2:
Subtle request.

Message 3:
Another employee responds.

Message 4:
Suspicious intention becomes clearer.

Message 5:
Employees discuss how to proceed.

This tests contextual detection.

17. ADVERSARIAL GENERATION

Generate challenging examples such as:

* Keyword present but harmless
* No keyword but suspicious meaning
* Suspicious wording but personal context
* Suspicious behavior spread across multiple messages
* Harmless beginning followed by suspicious content
* Similar-looking conversations with different outcomes

18. TEMPLATE-AWARE GENERATION

The source template determines:

* Number of participants
* Message count
* Reply pattern
* Thread depth
* CC behavior
* Forward behavior
* Writing style
* Message length
* Formatting

The scenario determines:

* What the employees are discussing
* What behavior they are engaging in
* Whether it should be alerting
* Difficulty
* Semantic variation

Core principle:

TEMPLATE = HOW THE COMMUNICATION LOOKS

SCENARIO = WHAT THE EMPLOYEES ARE DOING

19. RANDOMIZATION

Randomize:

* Synthetic identities
* Subjects
* Dates
* Message wording
* Conversation length
* Participant combinations
* Writing style
* Explicit vs implicit language
* Position of suspicious information
* Reply sequence

Avoid generating hundreds of conversations that look like copies of each other.

20. DISTRIBUTION CONTROL

If the source corpus contains:

60% one-to-one
25% three-person
10% four-person
5% larger groups

the generated dataset should approximately follow the communication structure distribution, with controlled variation.

The user should also be able to override this.

